Hard fork of bb 1.3rc1, the AA-project demo (https://aa-project.sourceforge.net/bb/)
Upstream tarball: bb-1.3rc1.tar.gz  sha256: 793d88c872793b6dab444cf5bab24f283ecb2f3502f1479ebbe41dd8e90b81c4
Frozen: no upstream merges. License: GPL-2.0 (see COPYING — kept, and AUTHORS
with it: the demo's own credits scroll is the authors' work and stays).

The tree that upstream ships is 81 files; this carries the 42 sources and 16
headers the binary is actually built from, the three .s3m tracks, and nothing
else. What went: the whole autotools apparatus (configure, Makefile.in,
aclocal.m4, install-sh, missing, mkinstalldirs, acconfig.h, aconfig.h.in,
stamp-h.in), which is replaced by a static src/aconfig.h and a build.sh that
calls the compiler; and ChangeLog, NEWS, INSTALL, bb.lsm, README.LZO.

KDOS changes, all applied in place because this is our source now:

  bb.c      timestuff() paces the DRAW on a DEADLINE GRID of one frame per
            16ms. A scene states a rate for its control and never for its
            picture, and one draw was 25ms on the hardware this was written
            for, so the loop paced itself; here it draws as fast as the
            machine turns it -- 5000 to 15000 frames a second in a fifty-
            column window, 17 MB/s of escape sequences for a display that
            shows sixty. The pty then never empties, every write comes apart
            mid-frame, and the terminal composes a window from a screen it
            has half received.
            THE GRID IS WHAT MAKES IT A CAP AT EVERY SCREEN SIZE. Timed from
            the END of a draw the period is 16ms PLUS the draw, and the draw
            grows with the screen -- 0.46ms at 80x25 against 1.35ms at
            240x67 -- so the period grows past the one thing it must stay
            under: 1000000/60, the interval fourteen scenes state for their
            control. A waitmode scene draws only on a turn its control fired,
            so a period over the control's refuses every other tick and the
            next chance is a whole interval later. Measured over 40s of one
            scene, end-of-draw against deadline: 59.1/49.1/31.0 fps at
            80x25/106x33/240x67 against 60.6/60.6/60.6, and frames held 25ms
            or longer 0.2%/23.5%/93.9% against 0.1% at all three. At 106x33
            there is nothing at all between 18ms and 30ms -- the bimodal
            16.7/33.3 alternation an eye reads as judder. It is a GATE and
            not a cost: one draw is 1.35ms against a 16ms budget.
            SIXTEEN, because 666us under 1000000/60 is the margin that keeps
            the deadline from ever being AHEAD of the control tick it is
            checked against, and because the consumers' floors differ -- the
            console session composes on CON_FRAME_MS = 16, while kdos-term on
            Wayland has no constant and takes the output's 16.667. A producer
            past a floor beats against it at the difference. Further under is
            surplus nobody composes, further over is the halved rate.
            A DEADLINE MORE THAN A WHOLE PERIOD BEHIND IS MOVED, never
            chased: a stall must cost one late frame and not a burst of every
            frame it missed. AND NOTHING TESTS THE GAP FOR GOING BACKWARDS --
            the int subtraction is modular, so the turnover reads as the
            small number it is (driven across it: 61.8 fps unbroken, no gap
            over 18ms), and the servo cannot produce a negative gap because
            its trim is capped at a fraction of the time that really passed.
            THE CONTROL KEEPS ITS OWN RATE -- a handler is told how many
            intervals it covers -- so no scene is a microsecond longer and
            the music stays in step: measured under a pty, stage 1+2 reaches
            the credits at 311.740156s against 311.740147s.
            EVERY FRAME IS BRACKETED IN SYNCHRONIZED OUTPUT (DECSET 2026) by
            bbflush(), and nothing calls aa_flush() directly. aalib hands the
            frame to ncurses' refresh(), which writes ONE write() PER SCREEN
            ROW -- 69 writes and 17.2 KB at 236x63 against a pty that holds
            12288 -- so a full-screen frame cannot cross in one piece however
            this is written, and a consumer composing on its own clock reads
            half of two frames. The bracket is the fix precisely because the
            write cannot be made atomic. It goes to stdout, the stream
            aalib's curses driver is initscr()'d on, with both ends flushed,
            and only when stdout is a terminal. The consumer half is
            kvt_term_sync_hold() under a 150ms watchdog, so a producer that
            sets the hold and dies cannot freeze a window, and a terminal
            that honours neither ignores a private mode it does not know.
            Measured at 236x63: 741 frames, 100.00% of bytes inside a
            bracket, zero nesting errors, biggest bracketed frame 20178 B.
  main.c    the mixer runs on its own thread. Upstream pumped MikMod_Update
            from a 10ms timer in the same group bbwait() pumps, and bbwait()'s
            caller has just blocked in aa_flush() writing the frame to the
            terminal — so under a compositor the audio starved whenever the
            window was actually being drawn, and was perfect when it was
            minimised. The thread sleeps to an ABSOLUTE deadline, so the
            update is inside the 10ms period rather than added to it, and a
            deadline already past is moved rather than chased -- catching up
            is a SCHED_FIFO thread that does not sleep. It records its worst
            unscheduled gap and never prints one: stderr here is the terminal
            the demo draws on, and a real-time thread blocked in write() is
            the very stall the instrument is looking for.
  tex.c     clear_zbuff() cleared sizeof(long) per cell against a sizeof(int)
            allocation. The same size in 1997, double on x86_64: the heap
            corrupts and stage 2 aborts on "malloc(): corrupted top size".
  messager.c the scroll used memcpy on overlapping ranges. glibc survived it;
            musl is free not to. memmove.
  config.h  REGISTERS(n) no longer expands to __attribute__((regparm(n))),
            which is x86-32 only and warns on every declaration here.

Then the rebranding, which is deliberate rather than incidental. The demo is
the AA-group's and the credits scroll, the greetings and the history stay
theirs; what changed is everything that would now be FALSE, plus the fork's
own mark in the two beats that already spend time on a logo.

  bb.c      the arguments are parsed as a list, and -nosound and -mixer join
            -loop. Usage names kdos-bb.
  main.c    THE DEMO STARTS BY ITSELF. Upstream asked "Music?[Y/n]" and then
            held the mixer settings table until Continue was pressed -- two
            questions whose answers were always yes and default, in front of
            the one thing the program does. Both are flags now.
            song_progress() is new: thousandths through the module, which is
            what paces the extro scroll.
  scene1.c  blazinec()'s nineteen flash words are reworded and the mascot is
            drawn in the twelfth, which upstream spent on an empty string.
            THE COUNT IS THE CLOCK -- one strobe pair each -- so the scene is
            the same length to the microsecond and the music stays in step.
  credits.c the same rule: text[] is 166 entries and CSIZE drives TOTAL, so
            entries are reworded and never added. Every contributor line and
            every Special Thanks is untouched; the fork's own credit takes
            the four slots after the last contributor, and the pointer that
            named README now names this file, which is the one that exists.
  credits2.c the mascot in the extro's logo beat, and THE CLOSING TEXT
            SCROLLS ITSELF, A SCREEN AT A TIME. It was a pager that waited on
            a keypress, so the demo ended by sitting still until somebody
            noticed. It now asks the player where it is and turns to the page
            that fraction of the way through, landing on the last page just
            before bb3.s3m ends. Any key hands control back for good -- a
            scroll that pulled the page out from under a reader would be
            worse than none. The module LOOPS (update_sound restarts it), so
            a fraction that went backwards is read as "the song is over".
            A SCREEN AND NEVER A LINE: every step of `p` costs a morph, a
            whole second of cross-fade, and the document is some fifteen
            screens against four and a half minutes -- a line at a time asks
            for one every three quarters of a second, the morph never lands,
            and nothing on the page can be read. Measured at 106x33: 16
            turns, 16.4s a page, 1s of it the morph.
  main.c    sound_sync() is a SERVO and not only a report: the scene clock
            follows the player. A scene runs to an absolute deadline, so time
            the machine loses is paid in skipped frames -- but the module
            advances one tick per tick inside the mixer, every rendered
            sample is written and the player is never seeked forward, so a
            moment the card spends not playing is a moment the picture took
            and the music did not, and the gap only ever grows.
            THE CORRECTION IS A RATE AND NOT A LUMP, because bb.c paces the
            next frame off the clock the correction bends: a step handed over
            whole lands inside ONE frame interval and that frame is as long
            as the step -- 10ms against a 16ms budget is a frame taking 27,
            and the corrections were 99.6% of the variance in the frame
            interval. So the error against module->sngtime is LOOKED AT every
            200ms and PAID OUT on every call, each call taking the slice the
            time since the previous one is worth, clamped to 5% of that same
            time. Measured on a replica over 280s of scene clock losing 85ms
            of audio every 3s: frame interval sd 1.802ms -> 0.377ms, p2p
            11.385 -> 5.773, doubled frames 1.81/s -> 0.00/s, 4.9
            corrections/s of 9883us -> 258.0/s of 162us (worst 10797us ->
            775us), and the drift unchanged at +0.100s. Over 1200s, +0.030s
            either way at sd 1.809ms against 0.379ms: the drift is BOUNDED
            and not merely small, and what varies run to run is the phase the
            track started at, which is one quantum and is kept on purpose.
            The limit has a
            floor, because what is rendered and what is played are not the
            same second and the emulated card this is tested against
            disagrees by 1.2% steadily; the phase each track started with is
            KEPT, because what is heard trails what is rendered by rings this
            program cannot measure.
            sngtime IS A STAIRCASE AND NOT A CLOCK, which is what the servo
            would otherwise saturate on: it moves one whole mixer buffer at a
            time -- a 90.0ms riser every 97.7ms at the shipped quantum, with
            88.3% of the mixer's 10ms ticks advancing it by nothing at all --
            so a servo fed the raw reading applied 5.37 corrections/s of
            7.74ms against a true disagreement of 0.12%. The reading is
            carried forward from the last step seen and AT MOST ONE RISER,
            past which the player has stopped rather than paused between
            buffers and the stall must stay visible; what is left is smoothed
            at 10% per look, about two seconds of memory. Across a quantum
            sweep of 0 to 170ms the frame sd stays 0.371-0.405ms, and a 500ms
            stall every 30s gives 0.481ms. THERE IS NO DEADBAND: a 2ms one
            buys no drift at all (+0.101s against +0.100s over 280s, +0.030s
            against +0.030s over 1200s) and costs the picture (p2p 18.941ms
            against 5.773ms, and 10.028 against 7.462), because forgiving a
            small error is forgiving the drift the servo exists to remove.
            IT REMOVES PHASE AND NOT ONLY RATE, which turns entirely on
            `base`: the rate would clear an error standing still in one
            interval, but the 5% clamp leaves the rest of a large one for the
            next look to carry, and `base` is what remembers it. Exactly
            two things may re-peg it -- a track that started or restarted, and
            the scene clock's int turnover -- and a third is a bug that shows
            as the demo ending seconds away from its music rather than as a
            bad frame. So the interval, the average and the turnover are all
            measured on the RAW reading, TIME plus everything the servo has
            handed tl_slowdown_timer(): a clock the servo is bending is a
            ruler the servo is stretching, so the trim under-pays by exactly
            the rate it is correcting. Measured over the same 280s, +11.980s
            of drift timed on TIME against +0.100s timed on the raw reading.
            And sound_clock() answers -1 until play() has started the player,
            because Player_Load() leaves sngtime at zero and so does the first
            tick -- bb.s3m is loaded 24s of scene clock before scene1() plays
            it, and a standing zero across that window reads as a player 24s
            behind. song_progress() is the wrong position for it -- its
            rows-per-pattern is nominal and its skew is the MODULE's: bb.s3m
            reads up to 14 thousandths high, bb3.s3m 13 low and shrinking,
            bb2.s3m 63 low and growing, and a measured table per track is in
            kdos-bb.md. Under KDOS_BB_DEBUG the line also carries `held`,
            the whole correction so far and therefore the audio time this
            machine has lost, and the mixer's worst unscheduled gap.
  textform.c the closing document gains what KDOS is and what this fork
            changed, and loses the claims that were no longer true.
  kdostux.c kdos.png as an LZO-compressed greyscale image in the format
            image.h describes, so dispimg() draws it with the same scaling,
            dithering and strobe as the photographs. GENERATED -- see
            genimg.py, which is host-only, checks its own round trip, and is
            never run by the build or on the target.
